-- Curated, human-reviewed self-test — same set served at every level.
with q as (
  select $l30_quiz$[{"id": "q1", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "What is the Drake equation actually for, and why is calling it a 'calculation' a mistake?", "options": ["It precisely computes how many aliens exist; we just need better telescopes", "It ORGANISES our ignorance \u2014 Frank Drake wrote it as the agenda for a three-day meeting, decomposing 'how many civilisations?' into seven individually-studiable factors and telling us exactly what we don't know; several factors are so uncertain that published estimates of N span ~14 orders of magnitude, so it produces no reliable number", "It proves the galaxy is full of life", "It is a law of physics like the rocket equation"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "N = R\u2217 \u00b7 f\u209a \u00b7 n\u2091 \u00b7 f\u2097 \u00b7 f\u1d62 \u00b7 f_c \u00b7 L. Drake wrote it in 1961 as the agenda for the first SETI meeting \u2014 a list of what to discuss \u2014 not a sum to be solved. Its value is that it breaks one impossibly vague question into seven specific ones and shows precisely what we'd need to learn. Because several factors are nearly unknown, plausible values give anything from millions of civilisations to far less than one \u2014 a span of about fourteen orders of magnitude. It is a framework for thinking, not a calculator."}, {"id": "q2", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "How has the astronomy of the last few decades changed the Drake equation, and which factors remain the deep unknowns?", "options": ["Every factor is now known; N is settled", "The first three factors are essentially CONQUERED \u2014 the star-formation rate is measured, nearly every star has planets, and ~1/5 of Sun-like stars have an Earth-sized planet in the habitable zone (the nearest ~4 light-years away) \u2014 so N is now dominated ENTIRELY by the unknown biological and civilisational terms: whether life arises (f\u2097), whether intelligence follows (f\u1d62), and above all the lifetime L", "The biology is now known but the astronomy is still a mystery", "None of the factors can ever be estimated"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "The exoplanet revolution settled the left-hand (astronomical) factors: star formation is measured, the fraction of stars with planets is essentially one, and roughly a fifth of Sun-like stars carry an Earth-sized world in the habitable zone \u2014 the stage for life is set abundantly. That leaves the whole answer riding on the terms we cannot measure: f\u2097 (does life arise?), f\u1d62 (does intelligence follow?), and L (how long does a civilisation last?). Because N is a product, any one of these, if tiny, drags N toward one \u2014 toward us, alone."}, {"id": "q3", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "Why may the lifetime L be the single factor that decides whether anyone is out there \u2014 and why does it 'turn the equation into a mirror'?", "options": ["Because longer-lived stars make more planets", "Because the galaxy holds detectable civilisations at any one moment only in proportion to how long each lasts \u2014 if technological species self-destruct within centuries, few ever overlap in time and the galaxy is silent tonight even if thousands have come and gone \u2014 and to guess how long OTHERS last we must guess how long WE will, entangling 'is anyone out there?' with 'will we survive ourselves?'", "Because L is the only factor we have actually measured", "Because it determines the speed of interstellar travel"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "The number of civilisations broadcasting simultaneously is roughly the total that ever arose times L divided by the galaxy's age. So a short L means almost no overlap \u2014 the galaxy can be dotted with the ruins of thousands and still be silent right now. That makes L potentially decisive. And it is a mirror: our only data point on the lifetime of a technological civilisation is ourselves, technological for barely a century, so estimating others' survival forces us to confront our own \u2014 the cosmic question becomes inseparable from the question of whether we destroy ourselves."}, {"id": "q4", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "State the Fermi paradox and the 'time argument' that makes it bite.", "options": ["Aliens exist but hide; the galaxy is too big to cross", "The paradox is the contradiction between 'they should be everywhere' and 'we see no one': the galaxy is ~13 billion years old, and a civilisation needs no faster-than-light travel to fill it \u2014 even at ~1% of light speed a probe crosses 100,000 light-years in ~10 million years, under a thousandth of the galaxy's age, so any expansionist civilisation has had time thousands of times over to reach every star, yet we detect nothing", "Faster-than-light travel is impossible, so no one can visit", "The galaxy is too young for life to have arisen yet"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Fermi's 'where is everybody?' is a quantitative contradiction. Plug even modest numbers into Drake and the galaxy should be crowded; add that it is billions of years old, with stars far older than the Sun, and that filling it needs only slow travel (a few million years even at a percent of light speed \u2014 a tiny fraction of the available time), and any civilisation with the will to expand should have left probes or traces everywhere, including here. Yet we observe silence \u2014 no artifacts, no megastructures, no confirmed signal. They should be everywhere; we see no one."}, {"id": "q5", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "What is the Great Filter, and what is its unsettling 'sting' regarding the discovery of alien life?", "options": ["A telescope that filters out noise; it has no bearing on our future", "The near-impossible step(s) on the road from dead matter to a galaxy-spanning civilisation that almost nothing passes (the paradox implies one must exist). If it is BEHIND us we are merely rare and our future is open; if AHEAD, civilisations reliably self-destruct and so may we. The sting: every step we find OTHERS also passed pushes the filter later, toward us \u2014 so finding abundant alien life is OMINOUS, and a sterile universe may be a reprieve", "A barrier that stops radio waves from crossing the galaxy", "The idea that only Earth could ever have formed"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Since almost nothing completes the road (or the galaxy would be full), at least one step must be fantastically improbable \u2014 the Great Filter. If that step is behind us (the origin of life, say), we passed a barrier that stopped everyone else \u2014 reassuring, our future open. If ahead (civilisations reliably die before they spread), the silence is that of countless dead predecessors and we approach the same wall. The sting inverts our hopes: discovering that life arises easily shows the earliest hard step is NOT the filter, making it likelier the filter lies ahead \u2014 so alien life is a warning, and dead rocks are the good news."}, {"id": "q6", "type": "open", "prompt": "The Great Filter's logic is seductive but not airtight. Explain the argument (the road, the filter, behind-vs-ahead, and the 'finding life is bad news' sting), then lay out the three strongest objections \u2014 that going quiet is not going extinct, that anthropic selection wrecks the inference, and that the silence may be a fact about our instruments \u2014 and explain what SETI's sixty years of near-silence does and does not establish.", "rubric": "THE ARGUMENT: the road from dead matter to a galaxy-spanning civilisation is a sequence of steps (habitable planet -> origin of life -> complex cells -> intelligence -> technology -> star-spanning expansion). The Fermi paradox implies almost NOTHING completes it (or the galaxy would be full and loud), so at least one step must be FANTASTICALLY IMPROBABLE: the Great Filter. BEHIND vs AHEAD: if the filter is BEHIND us (a hard step we already passed \u2014 e.g. the origin of life, or complex cells, or intelligence), we are simply RARE, the hard part is done, and our future is OPEN (good news). If AHEAD (a step we haven't reached \u2014 civilisations reliably self-destruct via war/collapse/lethal technology before they spread), the silence is that of countless dead predecessors and we approach the SAME wall (a prophecy of probable doom). THE STING: every rung we discover that OTHERS also climbed pushes the filter LATER, toward us. So finding abundant alien life (proof the origin of life is EASY, i.e. an early step is not the filter) makes it MORE likely the filter lies AHEAD -> ominous; finding the universe STERILE suggests an early step IS the filter (behind us) -> a reprieve. Hence, perversely, dead rocks are good news and alien life is a warning. THE THREE OBJECTIONS (where the received story is wrong/overstated): (1) GOING QUIET IS NOT GOING EXTINCT \u2014 the paradox equates silence with death, but a civilisation may simply STOP broadcasting: radio may be a brief adolescent phase, replaced by tight-beamed/wired/efficient communication we cannot overhear, so a galaxy of mature QUIET civilisations looks identical to a galaxy of graves; 'undetectable' is not 'dead,' so the filter inference (short L = extinction) may be unfounded. (2) ANTHROPIC SELECTION WRECKS THE INFERENCE \u2014 we can only ask this question from a rung our lineage happened to SURVIVE to, so an observer ALWAYS finds themselves past whatever filters they cleared; this observation-selection bias means we cannot cleanly read the true probability of the steps behind us (they look passable because we are here to note them), corrupting estimates of how hard those steps were and where the filter sits. (3) THE SILENCE MAY BE ABOUT OUR INSTRUMENTS, NOT THE UNIVERSE \u2014 sixty years of SETI has covered only a MINUSCULE slice of the search space: few stars, a few decades, narrow frequency bands, listening only for signal-types WE happen to use \u2014 like dipping a cup once into the ocean, finding no fish, and declaring the sea empty. WHAT SETI ESTABLISHES: in 60+ years, essentially nothing confirmed \u2014 the one famous ghost being the 1977 'WOW! SIGNAL' (a strong, narrow, seemingly artificial burst, never repeated or explained, an anomaly not a detection). This silence is REAL but, weighed against how little of the sky/frequencies/time we've searched, is NOT yet strong evidence that no one is out there \u2014 absence of evidence here is weak evidence of absence. A strong answer treats the Great Filter as a genuinely important idea whose scariest conclusion rests on assumptions (silence = death; unbiased inference; thorough search) that are each questionable, so the honest verdict is that the question remains OPEN, not that doom is proven."}, {"id": "q7", "type": "open", "prompt": "This is the finale \u2014 synthesise the whole course. Explain how the five units (the rocket equation; orbits and trajectories; telescopes and seeing; the price of human spaceflight; the search for life) are 'the beginning and end of a single story' pointed at the question 'are we alone?', what each answer would mean, and the honest reckoning about what the space age was actually for.", "rubric": "THE SINGLE STORY (the five units as one arc toward 'are we alone?'): (1) THE ROCKET EQUATION (Unit I) \u2014 Tsiolkovsky's tyranny (to move in space you must throw mass backward; leaving Earth is exponentially hard, demanding staging and high exhaust velocity) = learning HOW TO LEAVE THE CRADLE, the price of admission to the whole search. (2) ORBITS & TRAJECTORIES (Unit II) \u2014 motion under gravity, transfers, gravity assists = learning HOW TO TRAVEL BETWEEN THE WORLDS (what lets a probe cross to the ocean moons). (3) TELESCOPES & SEEING (Unit III) \u2014 the photon economy, optics, SPECTROSCOPY that reads composition from light = learning HOW TO SEE ACROSS DISTANCES WE CANNOT CROSS \u2014 the very capability that reads exoplanet atmospheres for biosignatures. (4) THE PRICE OF HUMAN SPACEFLIGHT (Unit IV) \u2014 the human payload, bodily decay in weightlessness, the radiation wall, life support, mission architecture, organisational failures that kill crews = learning WHAT IT COSTS TO GO OURSELVES, and why we send machines. (5) THE SEARCH FOR LIFE (Unit V) \u2014 what life is (undefinable from N=1), how it begins (easy or hard?), where it lives (far more places than thought \u2014 ocean moons), the specific worlds (Europa, Enceladus), and how we'd detect it (biosignatures, false positives) = learning WHAT WE ARE LOOKING FOR AND WHY. THE UNIFICATION: the rocket equation and the search for life are the BEGINNING and END of ONE story \u2014 a species on a small wet planet that looked up, asked 'are we alone?', and built the physics, machines, telescopes and science to find out; propulsion is the price of admission, telescopes the instruments, ocean worlds/exoplanets the targets, biosignatures the evidence, Drake/Fermi the framework, the Great Filter the stakes. 'From Tsiolkovsky to Enceladus' \u2014 from the equation that lets us leave to the ocean that might hold the answer. WHAT THE ANSWER MEANS EITHER WAY: IF ALONE \u2014 not a footnote but the ONLY place matter has woken up and become aware of itself, the only eyes/mind of the cosmos; every human act cosmically unique; a reason not for despair but for staggering PRECIOUSNESS and RESPONSIBILITY (consciousness's sole home must not fail). IF NOT ALONE \u2014 the end of cosmic solitude and, crucially, the second independent example (N=1 -> N=2 from the what-is-life chapter) that finally lets us learn what life IS by comparison and tells us the universe is DISPOSED toward life, not dead but for one spark \u2014 among the most important discoveries in history. THE HONEST RECKONING (what the space age was really for): the soaring 'reaching out from the cradle' narrative is partly told afterward; the machines were NOT mostly built to answer the great question \u2014 APOLLO flew because of COLD WAR geopolitics (superpower fear and prestige, winning a contest without a war), flags-and-footprints, not a search for life. That honesty does NOT diminish what remains: the capability those machines left behind is now genuinely turned toward the question (probes at ocean worlds, telescopes reading atmospheres, dishes at the silence), and the search has matured enough to impose PLANETARY PROTECTION \u2014 refusing to contaminate a world we might study, sterilising and even destroying craft (as Cassini was destroyed) so we don't answer the greatest question with our own stowaway microbes; to search responsibly is to protect what we might find, even from ourselves. CLOSE: we do NOT yet know if we are alone, but we have turned the oldest wondering into a rigorous ONGOING science with real tools, targets, and a real prospect of an answer within living lifetimes \u2014 the question made rigorous, the search joined. A strong answer ties it all together as one arc and is candid about the geopolitics without cynicism, landing on the maturity (the ethic, not the prestige) that makes the search worthy of the question."}]$l30_quiz$::jsonb as questions
)
insert into quizzes (node_id, level, questions, generated)
select 'astronomy-and-space/space-exploration/astrobiology/drake-and-fermi', lvl, q.questions, true
from q, (values ('easy'), ('advanced'), ('expert')) as l(lvl)
on conflict (node_id, level) do update
  set questions = excluded.questions, generated = excluded.generated;
